WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.330
- [Narrator] TikTok and Walmart

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:03.670
may seem like an unlikely match,

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:06.470
but right now, Walmart is
one of the U.S. companies

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:11.018
vying to buy a portion of
TikTok, which has dance videos.

00:00:11.018 --> 00:00:12.010
(upbeat music)

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:15.148
Walmart has deals for customers.

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:16.330
- Whoop whoop!

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:18.030
- [Narrator] The potential
TikTok-Walmart deal

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.720
would satisfy the Trump
administration's new requirement

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.450
that TikTok be owned by Americans.

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:26.520
The sometimes viral,
short-form, video-sharing app

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.400
is currently owned by China's ByteDance.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.740
- It was really something to me

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:33.350
that was not on my bingo card
for this year for Walmart,

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:34.960
which makes it really interesting.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.450
It shows yet another
way that they are trying

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:44.220
to become more relevant to
shoppers today than Amazon.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.080
- [Narrator] There's a
Walmart within 10 miles

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.090
of 90% of Americans.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:50.470
Billions of people shop
at Walmart globally,

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:53.080
and it's the country's largest employer.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.710
But online, Walmart can't keep up

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.040
with Amazon's digital presence.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.487
This last quarter, Amazon posted a record

00:01:00.487 --> 00:01:04.250
$96.2 billion in sales.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:08.130
- That's why you see Walmart
just scrambling to grow online

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:12.170
and grow competence in terms
of getting people things fast,

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:13.810
or offering a way to buy online

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:15.830
and pickup in a store parking lot.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:18.210
They keep trying to figure out their way

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:22.590
to do something that even Amazon can't do.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:23.870
- [Narrator] Enter TikTok.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.420
If all goes well, the
deal could mean that users

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.550
might eventually be able to buy items

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:31.645
fulfilled by Walmart in TikTok videos.

00:01:31.645 --> 00:01:34.460
- Look at this! What is this
magical brush that he is using?

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.400
- [Narrator] Imagine being
able to buy this brush

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.250
without leaving the app
or even pausing the video.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:41.860
Could this acquisition
finally give Walmart

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:43.830
an edge over Amazon?

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.700
And how might this deal
transform online shopping

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:48.410
as we know it?
(light saber humming)

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.880
TikTok and Walmart are
not the only companies

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.430
to see marketing potential
in user-created videos.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:58.730
Instagram just integrated
shoppable features into IGTV,

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:01.380
and this kind of
recommendation-based video shopping

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:05.740
is already wildly popular on
apps, like Douyin, in China.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.430
- Douyin has recently announced

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:10.730
they've got 600 million
daily active users,

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:14.880
which means most internet users in China

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.600
open Douyin every day.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.240
- [Narrator] Matthew Brennan analyzes

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.400
the Chinese mobile internet sector,

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.890
and Douyin is ByteDance's
Chinese version of TikTok.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:27.280
It already has some of the
video commerce features

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.810
that Walmart could try to
imitate with TikTok in America.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:31.643
- [Matthew] They see the video

00:02:31.643 --> 00:02:33.210
and then they can just tap to buy.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:34.820
That's one form,

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:37.000
and the other form is
livestream e-commerce,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.520
where people will, rather
than watch short videos,

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.550
they'll watch live stream.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:42.383
- [Narrator] In the U.S.,

00:02:42.383 --> 00:02:45.560
Amazon launched Amazon
Live a few months ago.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:46.740
- We've linked it for you

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:48.330
so you can definitely check it out.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:49.550
- [Narrator] It gives influencers

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.240
a streaming video platform
to convince consumers

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.690
to buy their products in real time.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:55.610
- I'm grabbing those deals,

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:57.450
so you gotta grab those too.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.810
- [Narrator] American
brands like Tommy Hilfiger

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:03.300
are also experimenting with
shoppable live streams.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.770
- Always need a statement puffer.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:06.220
- [Narrator] But it's
still very early days

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.250
for any form of video commerce in the U.S.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:10.940
- And we want you to get involved.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.830
- [Narrator] Developing
these new features in a way

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:15.500
that aligns with current
shoppers' expectations

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.190
and behaviors can be a challenge.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:19.870
- Walmart buying TikTok
is definitely something

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:23.570
that is really different
and could leapfrog

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:27.340
what Amazon is currently
doing in some ways,

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:28.560
but of course they have to do it,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.830
and they have to do it in
a way that consumers like

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:33.050
and is seamless, and they
have to build the technology

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:33.900
on the backend.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.210
And those are all hard things to do.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:37.360
- [Narrator] And even technical

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.290
and logistical hurdles aside,

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:42.310
some experts warn that a
successful marketing strategy

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:44.770
in one market doesn't always translate

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:46.700
to success in another.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.140
- I think a lot of people assume
that the model for commerce

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:53.300
in China could be described
as the future of commerce

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.090
and could be taken as a better,

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.230
more modern approach to shopping.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.780
I think it's just a different
approach to shopping,

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:05.167
and transplanting it into
Europe as well as in the U.S.

00:04:05.167 --> 00:04:07.910
is not as straightforward
as most would assume.

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:10.410
- [Narrator] Joe Kaziukenas
collects and analyzes

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:12.580
leading commerce marketplace data.

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:15.330
He says that buying items
influencers are recommending

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:17.670
in videos that you're
watching for entertainment

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.590
is a more passive shopping experience

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:22.120
than actively searching on Etsy or Amazon

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.850
for something you've already
decided you want to own

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:26.420
before logging in.

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:30.100
- This assumption that
video shopping is the future

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.990
is being based purely on the fact

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.940
that it looks more
exciting, looks more modern.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.690
It looks like it's taking
on the sort of features

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.150
modern phones as well as websites allow.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:43.780
But that assumption
doesn't hold much ground

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:48.210
because the shopping we have
in the West is search-based,

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:51.390
and changing that
drastically is gonna take

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:52.830
a very, very long time.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:54.920
- [Narrator] Facebook and
Amazon declined to comment,

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.700
and Walmart didn't respond
to requests for comment.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:58.990
- It remains to be seen,

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:02.270
whether U.S. consumers are
ready to buy a lot of products

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:05.180
through a social media app,
but the social media companies,

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:06.950
the retailers, the brands,

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:09.240
they're betting that we already
discover a lot of products

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:12.190
that way through ads and
influencers and things like that,

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:16.030
and that we're gonna be ready
to buy if we aren't already.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:17.390
- [Narrator] And for good reason.

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:20.060
There are over 100 million
monthly American users

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:23.740
on TikTok, and over 100
million American households

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.670
shop at Walmart at least once a year.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:30.570
But experts say what makes each
popular in their own spaces

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:32.850
is no guarantee a partnership would create

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:34.410
a video commerce experience

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:38.207
enticing enough to change
how Americans already shop.

00:05:38.207 --> 00:05:40.790
(upbeat music)

